# Machine-local files this repository names by path but never commits. Each path is a
# value in harness.config.json, so it is the same for everyone who clones, while the file
# it names never leaves the machine. Where one of these has a committed `.example`
# sibling, that sibling is what a fresh clone copies from. The client env file is the one whose
# DIRECTORY is the configured value (`appDir`) while its name is fixed: setup-worktree.sh symlinks
# it into every working copy it bootstraps, so without this rule every fresh worktree opens with an
# untracked entry. What is ignored is the file and never the directory, so a committed
# `.env.example` beside it stays committable.
{{pushEnvPath}}
{{qaCredentialsPath}}
{{clientEnvPath}}
# The per-machine settings file the agent runner writes beside the committed profiles.
.claude/settings.local.json
# The presence-only marker one user's "don't ask again" answer writes to silence the change-request
# offer. It lives at the main worktree's root and is checked there from every worktree of this
# checkout, so one answer covers them all - its existence is the whole signal, so it has no
# contents to commit.
.claude/harness-no-offer
# The run daemon's transcripts, event logs and run registry are machine-local. The
# directory itself is committed, because its README is the contract for what belongs in
# it, so the contents are ignored and that one file is excepted - an ignore rule on the
# directory would take the README with it, and git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded.
{{logsGlob}}
{{logsReadmeException}}
# The unattended loop's stop, pause and dispatch-count control files, written flat at the root of
# the run-artifact tree while a run is in flight. The tree around them is committed, so one
# `git add` of it would take whichever exist at that moment - and a committed STOP halts every run
# for everyone who clones, at a step whose own instruction forbids deleting the file.
{{runControlArtifacts}}
# The questions a parked run asks and the answers it is given are machine-local, while the
# directory's README is the committed contract for the exchange - so the contents are ignored and
# that one file is excepted, exactly as for the log directory above. A question sits one level
# further down, under a <branch> subdirectory, which this glob covers with everything else beneath
# the directory. On a repository whose ignore file was generated before this pair existed: the
# managed block is only ever added to and never pruned, so the older whole-directory rule for this
# channel - the line ending `/clarifications/` - is still in the file and has to be DELETED BY HAND,
# and so do the two comment lines that stood above it, which state that this channel has no committed
# README and are wrong now that it has one. While that rule stands the exception cannot take effect,
# wherever the two sit relative to each other, because git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded - `doctor`'s `ignore-rules` check reports that state and names every line to
# delete.
{{clarificationsGlob}}
{{clarificationsReadmeException}}
# The drop point the unattended loop takes its work from. The prompts left in it are machine-local
# and its README is the committed contract for what belongs there, so the contents are ignored and
# that one file is excepted, exactly as for the log directory above.
{{inboxGlob}}
{{inboxReadmeException}}
# The throwaway files an agent runs a probe or a mutation check from. They are written to be executed
# once and deleted, never to be committed or read back, while the directory's README is the committed
# contract for that - so the contents are ignored and that one file is excepted, exactly as for the
# log directory above.
{{scratchGlob}}
{{scratchReadmeException}}
# The backup `config set` copies the configuration to before every write, and the one .bak an
# adopter gets without asking for it: applying a layer split is the documented path, so without this
# rule a wired repository carries an untracked file from its first `config set` onward. The leading
# slash anchors it at the repository root, the only place the configuration is written.
/{{configBackupFile}}
{{qaBrowserArtifacts}}
